<html><head><meta content="text/html; charset=UTF-8" http-equiv="content-type"></head><body class="doc-content" style="background-color:#ffffff;max-width:468pt;padding:72pt 72pt 72pt 72pt"><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">WORLD FOOTBALL &middot; REPORTED &amp; ANALYSED</span></p><h1 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:24pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:24pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Weekly Soccer Digest</span></h1><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Six leagues, one continent&rsquo;s worth of noise, and the paper trail behind it</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Four of Europe&rsquo;s big five are still in pre-season &mdash; La Liga opens Saturday, Serie A and Ligue 1 the following week, the Bundesliga not until 28 August. So this is a transfer-market edition by necessity, and it is a violent one: a Real Madrid club record, a Newcastle dismantling, a Barcelona balance-sheet rescue and a Dortmund&ndash;K&ouml;ln staring contest over &euro;55m. Arsenal are covered in their own companion edition and are carved out here. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Correction to earlier practice:</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;figures are given in the currency each club or reporter used; where outlets disagree, both numbers appear.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">THE LEAD</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Reset at the Bern&aacute;beu</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">In one August afternoon Real Madrid broke their transfer record, ended the biggest contract standoff in the game and handed Jos&eacute; Mourinho a squad built to win the league back. What they still do not have is the one player he actually asked for &mdash; and he appears to be going to Barcelona.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">MADRID &middot; ANALYSIS</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>On Thursday 6 August, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Real Madrid</span><span>&nbsp;published two official communiqu&eacute;s within hours of one another. The first confirmed that </span><span style="font-weight:700">Yan Diom&aacute;nde</span><span>, a nineteen-year-old Ivorian winger, had signed from </span><span style="font-weight:700">RB Leipzig</span><span>&nbsp;on a seven-year contract running to 30 June 2033 &mdash; the club&rsquo;s own wording was that he &ldquo;will be linked to our club for the next seven seasons.&rdquo; The second confirmed that </span><span style="font-weight:700">Vin&iacute;cius J&uacute;nior</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;had extended to 30 June 2032. Madrid disclosed no fee for the first and no salary for the second, which is the modern way of announcing that you have just spent more money than you ever have.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The Diom&aacute;nde number is where the reporting fractures. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Eurosport</span><span>&nbsp;put it at &euro;130m; </span><span style="font-weight:700">RPP</span><span>&nbsp;and several Spanish outlets at &euro;125m; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Semana</span><span>&nbsp;at &euro;140m all-in with variables; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Football365</span><span>&rsquo;s sterling table has it at &pound;107m. The safe formulation &mdash; and the one this paper will use &mdash; is a base of roughly &euro;125&ndash;130m rising toward &euro;140m with add-ons, which makes it the most expensive signing in Real Madrid&rsquo;s history and the third-largest fee of the entire 2026 window. He is a player with exactly one season of top-five-league football behind him. He arrived at Leipzig from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Legan&eacute;s</span><span>&nbsp;only twelve months ago, in what remains that club&rsquo;s record sale, then produced twelve goals and eight assists and was named the Bundesliga&rsquo;s young player of the season. </span><span style="font-weight:700">PSG</span><span>&nbsp;walked away over the fee. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Liverpool</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;had a June bid rejected.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>The Vin&iacute;cius half of the day is the more consequential piece of business, because it closed a saga that had run for more than a year. He had entered the final twelve months of his deal earning a reported &euro;15&ndash;17m net; several Premier League clubs had done the arithmetic on matching it and concluded they could not. Madrid, with </span><span style="font-weight:700">Florentino P&eacute;rez</span><span>&nbsp;personally involved, made a final offer and let the deadline do the work. The club&rsquo;s statement described a player who &ldquo;has become one of the most important players in one of the most successful stages of our history.&rdquo; Vin&iacute;cius, on Instagram, was less corporate: </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">&ldquo;Eight years at the Bern&aacute;beu is a short time, six more years and forever!&rdquo;</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f2e22;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&ldquo;Eight years at the Bern&aacute;beu is a short time, six more years and forever!&rdquo;</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">VIN&iacute;CIUS J&uacute;NIOR, ON SIGNING TO 2032</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>All of it sits underneath a manager who has been in the job nine weeks. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Mourinho</span><span>&nbsp;was appointed on 11 June 2026 &mdash; the club&rsquo;s communiqu&eacute; specifies a three-year deal &ldquo;hasta el 30 de junio de 2029&rdquo; &mdash; and reported for pre-season on 13 July. He is the third man in the dugout inside eight months: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Xabi Alonso</span><span>&nbsp;was dismissed on 12 January, </span><span style="font-weight:700">&aacute;lvaro Arbeloa</span><span>&nbsp;took the season out as interim and has since gone to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fulham</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. It is Mourinho&rsquo;s second Madrid spell, thirteen years after the first ended, and he inherits a club that has now gone two consecutive seasons without a major trophy &mdash; a sentence that reads like a misprint and is not.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The tactical objection writes itself. Madrid now have </span><span style="font-weight:700">Kylian Mbapp&eacute;</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Jude Bellingham</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Vin&iacute;cius</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Diom&aacute;nde</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; four players whose best work happens in the same two half-spaces, none of whom has ever been asked to defend a full-back for ninety minutes by a coach with any leverage. What Mourinho reportedly asked the board for was not another forward. It was a deep-lying organiser to structure the build-up, the single position in which this squad has been thin since </span><span style="font-weight:700">Toni Kroos</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;retired. Madrid have spent the summer chasing exactly that, and this week they appear to have lost.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Rodri</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; Spain&rsquo;s captain, a world champion, and the man who took the Golden Ball at the tournament in July &mdash; has, on the reporting of the last seven days, chosen </span><span style="font-weight:700">Barcelona</span><span>&nbsp;over Madrid. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Goal</span><span>&nbsp;reported the Madrid strand frozen: Rodri wanted four years, Madrid&rsquo;s wage offer &ldquo;fell well short.&rdquo; By 11 August </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fabrizio Romano</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;had it that the Barcelona deal was &ldquo;advancing very well,&rdquo; that the fee would exceed &euro;60m and that &ldquo;all parties are confident.&rdquo; That is a remarkable thing for Madrid to have permitted: the outstanding defensive midfielder of his generation, in the final year of his contract, moving to the one club they most did not want him at, in a summer when they were willing to spend &euro;130m on a teenager.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Zoom out and the Madrid spend looks less like an outlier than a symptom. This is a window in which the most expensive British footballer in history &mdash; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Morgan Rogers</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Aston Villa</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Chelsea</span><span>&nbsp;for &pound;117m &mdash; is only the biggest deal by a nose, with </span><span style="font-weight:700">Elliot Anderson</span><span>&rsquo;s &pound;116m move from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nottingham Forest</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester City</span><span>&nbsp;a whisker behind. It is a window in which </span><span style="font-weight:700">Newcastle</span><span>&nbsp;have sold three first-choice players for roughly &pound;236m and in which the Saudi Pro League&rsquo;s largest outlay is &pound;55m &mdash; a sharp cooling on 2023. It is also a window playing out under UEFA&rsquo;s 70 per cent squad-cost cap, which bit hard enough on </span><span style="font-weight:700">Strasbourg</span><span>&nbsp;in July to produce a &euro;13m fine. Real Madrid, notably, registered </span><span style="font-weight:700">Denzel Dumfries</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Marc Cucurella</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ibrahima Konat&eacute;</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;with LaLiga without recourse to any of the financial levers that have defined Barcelona&rsquo;s last four summers.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>And then there is the calendar, which has quietly done Mourinho a favour. Because Spain reached and won the World Cup final, LaLiga postponed four of the six opening-weekend fixtures. Real Madrid&rsquo;s scheduled home game against </span><span style="font-weight:700">Real Sociedad</span><span>&nbsp;has been pushed to Wednesday 26 August; their first competitive match of the Mourinho era is now away at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Espanyol</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;on Saturday 22 August. That is ten more days to integrate a &euro;130m nineteen-year-old, to decide who among four attackers is dropped, and to find out whether the midfield they wanted is instead going to be lining up against them at the Camp Nou in October.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">KEY FACTS &middot; REAL MADRID&rsquo;S SUMMER LEDGER</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">IN &mdash;</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight:700">Yan Diom&aacute;nde</span><span>&nbsp;(RB Leipzig, c.&euro;125&ndash;130m rising to c.&euro;140m, to 2033) &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Marc Cucurella</span><span>&nbsp;(Chelsea, reported &pound;47.5m) &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Denzel Dumfries</span><span>&nbsp;(Inter) &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Carlos Esp&iacute;</span><span>&nbsp;(Levante) &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bernardo Silva</span><span>&nbsp;(Manchester City, free) &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ibrahima Konat&eacute;</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;(Liverpool, free).</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;height:11pt;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal"></span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">OUT &mdash;</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight:700">Dani Carvajal</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">David Alaba</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Dani Ceballos</span><span>&nbsp;released &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Gonzalo Garc&iacute;a</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">C&eacute;sar Palacios</span><span>&nbsp;to Fulham &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fran Garc&iacute;a</span><span>&nbsp;to Real Betis &middot; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Franco Mastantuono</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;on a dry season loan to Fiorentina, Fiorentina covering roughly 60 per cent of salary.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;height:11pt;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal"></span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">STAYING &mdash;